### Tested versions

- Reproducible in:
v4.4.stable.steam [4c311cbee]
v4.4.beta4.official [93d270693]
v4.4.beta3.official [06acfccf8]
v4.4.beta2.official [a013481b0]
v4.4.beta1.official [d33da79d3]

- Not reproducible in 
v4.4.dev7.official [46c8f8c5c]
v4.3.stable.official [77dcf97d8]

### System information

Godot v4.4.stable (4c311cbee) - Windows 10 (build 19045) - Multi-window, 1 monitor - Vulkan (Forward+) - dedicated NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060 (NVIDIA; 32.0.15.7270) - AMD Ryzen 5 7600X 6-Core Processor (12 threads)

### Issue description

I just updated my project to 4.4 and everything works fine, except my own shader looks a bit different. The specular highlights in my custom shader were all messed up. My specular uses the NORMAL built-in value for calculating this highlight, and I figured out that I needed to normalize it in Godot 4.4 like so: `normalize(NORMAL)`

In Godot 4.3 , I did not need to normalize this value. But in Godot 4.4 I need to normalize it in order to make it look the same. I am using the forward+ renderer.

Here is how it looks on my player model. When I normalize the value, it looks like Godot 4.3:

![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/e4d5afc0-541b-4ab6-a629-624c6ffacd12)

According to u/godot_clayjohn on Reddit, this may be a bug associated with this change in the normal mapping code: [https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/100441/](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/100441/)

Here is my shader code that uses the NORMAL built-in to calculate world normals (more on this in reproducing/MRP):

```glsl
	vec3 WORLD_NORMAL = (INV_VIEW_MATRIX * vec4(NORMAL, 0.0)).xyz;
	vec3 WORLD_NORMAL_NORMALIZED = (INV_VIEW_MATRIX * vec4(normalize(NORMAL), 0.0)).xyz;
```

### Steps to reproduce

Included in my MRP is a shader (MyShader_01) that uses the NORMAL built-in to calculate the world normal. I then use the world normal to do a basic dot product with a light direction to give me a stylized highlight effect (that is always aligned slightly offset from the view's direction). In the shader is a uniform (normalizeSwitch) to swap between using the normalized and non-normalized NORMAL. 

I also have a scene with a torus mesh with a material using the shader applied. When using non-normalized NORMAL, the highlight has sharp corners and looks polygon-esque. When using a normalized NORMAL, the highlight is smooth and round. In Godot 4.3, these would both look the same.

![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/32105ad0-8c51-40f3-b063-40c808fccca6)


Here is my shader code below:
```glsl
shader_type spatial;
render_mode blend_mix, unshaded;

uniform vec3 albedoColor : source_color = vec3(1, 0, 0);

uniform float highlightCutoff : hint_range(0.8, 1.0, 0.01) = .98;

/** Use this uniform to switch between normalized and non-normalized NORMAL.
0 is normalized and 1 is non normalized. In 4.3 these would look the same. But in 4.4,
the NORMAL built-in value is no longer normalized by default, causing the non-normalized
highlight to have sharp corners and look polygon-esque. */
uniform float normalizeSwitch : hint_range(0.0, 1.0, 1.0) = 0.0;

const vec3 highlightColor = vec3(1, 1, 1);
const vec4 lightDirection = vec4(normalize(vec3(0.5, 0.5, 1.0)), 0.0);

void fragment() {
	vec3 WORLD_NORMAL = (INV_VIEW_MATRIX * vec4(NORMAL, 0.0)).xyz;
	vec3 WORLD_NORMAL_NORMALIZED = (INV_VIEW_MATRIX * vec4(normalize(NORMAL), 0.0)).xyz;
	vec3 VIEW_LIGHT_NORMAL = (INV_VIEW_MATRIX * lightDirection).xyz;

	float lightValue = smoothstep(dot(WORLD_NORMAL, VIEW_LIGHT_NORMAL), highlightCutoff, 1.0);
	lightValue = clamp(lightValue, 0.0, 1.0);

	float lightValueNormalized = smoothstep(dot(WORLD_NORMAL_NORMALIZED, VIEW_LIGHT_NORMAL), highlightCutoff, 1.0);
	lightValueNormalized = clamp(lightValueNormalized, 0.0, 1.0);

	float finalMixValue = mix(lightValueNormalized, lightValue, normalizeSwitch);

	ALBEDO = mix(highlightColor, albedoColor, finalMixValue);
}

```

